# Moving [[ dist_name ]] into a canvodpy monorepo

This repository is canvodpy-extensions reduced to one package, so the
package moves without changes. The canVODpy maintainers decide whether it
moves; open an issue in the target repository first.

## What moves, what stays

| Moves | Stays here (the target has its own) |
|---|---|
| `packages/[[ dist_name ]]/` (code, tests, `pyproject.toml`, `README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`) | `.github/`, `tools/`, `scripts/`, `.claude/settings.json`, hooks, root `pyproject.toml`, `Justfile`, `uv.lock` |
| `docs/packages/[[ short_name ]]/`, `docs/api/[[ dist_name ]].md` | `docs/index.md`, `docs/releasing.md`, `docs/agents/`, `zensical.toml` |
| `release-notes/[[ dist_name ]]-v*.py` | `release-notes/_template.py`, `README.md` |

## Into canvodpy-extensions

Keep the history of the package folder with `git subtree`:

```bash
cd canvodpy-extensions
git switch -c feat/[[ short_name ]]
git subtree add --prefix=packages/[[ dist_name ]] [[ repo_url ]].git <branch-of-a-split>
```

where the split branch is made here first:

```bash
git subtree split --prefix=packages/[[ dist_name ]] -b split-[[ short_name ]]
git push --no-follow-tags origin split-[[ short_name ]]
```

Then follow the extensions' own guide for a new package,
`.claude/skills/add-package/SKILL.md` there ("Register it"): root
`pyproject.toml` test paths and coverage, `zensical.toml` nav, the docs
pages, the package tables in `README.md` and `AGENTS.md`, the commit
scope in `CONTRIBUTING.md`. Change the project URLs in
`packages/[[ dist_name ]]/pyproject.toml` to canvodpy-extensions. The old
releases and their tags `[[ dist_name ]]-v*` stay in this repository; the
target continues with the next version under the same tag prefix.

## Into canvodpy

Same steps into canvodpy's `packages/`, then canvodpy's own checklist
(its root `AGENTS.md`). canvodpy's packages share its workspace, version
bounds `<2`, the umbrella `canvodpy` pin and its API levels: a package
there may need changes to canvodpy's API and CLI. Plan that with the
maintainers first.
